```javascript
function findNestedEntry(obj, key) {
  const keys = key.split("."); // Split the input string into an array of keys
  let current = obj; // Set the current object to the initial object passed as an argument

  for (const subKey of keys) {
    if (current[subKey] && typeof current[subKey] === "object") {
      current = current[subKey]; // If the current property is an object, set it as the new current object
    } else if (current[subKey] === undefined) {
      return undefined; // If we reach a property that doesn't exist, return undefined
    } else {
      break; // If we have found the matching key-value pair, we can stop iterating
    }
  }

  return current; // Return the matched entry
}
```

This function uses recursion to traverse the object structure based on the dot-notated keys in the `key` parameter. It splits the key string into an array of subkeys and iterates through each subkey until it finds the matching nested property or reaches the end of the object hierarchy.

