 To prevent the planets from being generated too close to each other, you can modify the `generateRandomPlanets` function by keeping track of the distances between the newly generated planet and all existing planets, instead of checking only their positions. Here's an updated version of the function:

```typescript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
 const planets = []
 let attempts = 0

 while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
   attempts++

   const planet = {
     position: [
       randomPosition(-30, 30),
       randomPosition(-30, 30),
       randomPosition(-30, 30),
     ],
     size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
     color: randomColor(),
     ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
     ringColor: randomColor(),
     ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
   }

   let tooClose = false

   for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
     const distance = Math.sqrt(
       Math.pow(planet.position[0] - planets[i].position[0], 2) +
       Math.pow(planet.position[1] - planets[i].position[1], 2) +
       Math.pow(planet.position[2] - planets[i].position[2], 2)
     );

     if (distance < minDistance) {
       tooClose = true;
       break;
     }
   }

   if (!tooClose) {
     planets.push(planet);
   }
 }

 return planets;
}
```

This updated function checks the distance between the newly generated planet and all existing ones using the Euclidean distance formula. If the distance is less than the minimum distance, it continues generating new planets until it finds one that is far enough away. This should help ensure that the planets are evenly spaced out.

